A||||22||eagle01||torque reaction strut||||5||
Z||000000||eagle01||12-15-1999||03:34 AM||||what would happen if i were to run my 85 fiero v-6 without the torque reaction strut?<BR>would it being disconnected ruin anything in the drivetrain or suspension?  or would it just create torque steer, albeit in the rear?<BR>do the v-8 conversions even make accomodations for torque reaction struts?<BR>thanks in advance for your responses.<P>eagle01||||reg||5
Z||000001||Carrolles||12-15-1999||06:15 AM||||Since the engine is transverse mounted there would be no torque steer effect.  However you would be placing excessive forces on the engine and transmission mounts and they would eventually fail.  All of the mounts (at least on my 85 SE v-6 4 spd) are rubber type mounts with bolts or studs.  Excessive movement causes the bolts/studs to tear free from the rubber leaving the engine/trans just sitting on the supports.<P>During acceleration and braking the engine would rock back and forth.  You would feel this movement in the car.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||12-15-1999||06:26 AM||||What if you went with aluminum engine bushings and no dogbone?||||reg||1
Z||000003||DJRice||12-15-1999||09:33 AM||||Yup, What Carrolles Said.  You will quickly ruin all other motor mounts in the car.  Every time you start you will hear the slam of your engine colliding with your trunk.  Possibly damaging exhaust manifold, alternator, cruse control (if equipped).  When you stop you will be banging the other exhaust manifold against the firewall again taking the chance of ruining the manifold, maybe AC Compressor.<P>Why would you not want the Torque Strut in place?||||reg||1
Z||000004||william_thorniii||12-15-1999||07:24 PM||||Monkey,<P>The aluminum bushings would take care of the shear movement (i.e. fore and aft shifting/sliding).  The negative effects would be due to torque.  Think about a breaker bar or flex handle depending on what you Hoosiers call it.  Naturally you can exert more force when the distance increases from the center line of the bolt.  Well this would happen with your engine.  Would it cause any problems?  Probably not initially, but I wouldn't reccomend it.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||12-16-1999||02:01 AM||||I hadn't thought of it that way.  BTW--we call it a breaker bar over here in the Hoosier (best damn state in the country!).||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||12-16-1999||07:12 AM||||If by aluminum engine bushings you mean replacing the rubber engine/transmission mounts with solid aluminum mounts then you would not need a dogbone strut but I don't think you would be happy with the results.<P>If by aluminum bushings you mean the bushings between the cradle and the frame then you will still have the rubber type engine/transmission mounts between the engine/transmission and the cradle.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Wolfhound||12-16-1999||07:22 AM||||I would imagine if you made the dog bone solid you would transfer all the engine vibration to the subframe. Not good, but you could find all your rattles.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||12-16-1999||11:49 AM||||I remember, in an old thread, that some one made dogbone bushings out of oak.  I'd think that *some* give would be necessary otherwise you'd be in danger of cracking things?  Tell me if I'm wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000009||deceler8||12-16-1999||12:58 PM||||I'm the oak bushing guy. To combat the vibration, I added a piece of 1/2" heater hose in the center of the bushing and it worked pretty well.<P>But then I went to <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com" TARGET=_blank>www.suspension.com</A>  and discovered that poly dogbone bushings were only $17, so I trashed the wood and went with the poly. The reason I originally experimented with wood was the high price the Fiero Store wanted for the poly bushings.<P>Poly transmits a lot more vibration than rubber, but now there's less noise from the motor shifting during accel/decel.||||reg||11
Z||000010||william_thorniii||12-16-1999||07:30 PM||||Monkey,<BR>The idea is to dissipate vibration... you are correct, essentially without doing so, every time you turn your car on, you will stress test all welds.  There is a happy medium for road cars of dampening vs. deflection.  That is why poly bushings are often used.  They provide good dampening qualities yet keep the engine deflection to a minimum. <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000011||DJRice||12-17-1999||09:50 AM||||I recently got a dogbone out of my 85, upgraded it to the poly bushings, and then put it in my 87.  It did increase vibration a bit, but all in all I like the results.  The engine moves around a whole lot less.  <P>I was going to head for Home Depot some time and look for something to make my own rubber bushings but if they polys are down to $17 at suspension.com Ill just get another set from them (for a different car)||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||12-17-1999||02:44 PM||||OK, I guess I'm a little confused.  I thought that the "torque reaction strut" was the same as the "dogbone".  Apparently not.  I know what and where the dogbone is and what it does.  What's the torque reaction strut and where is it.||||reg||1
Z||000013||william_thorniii||12-17-1999||06:39 PM||||Monkey,<P>I was confused, according to my 22p, I had previously been thinking of #36 on page 0-28.  Which is called the ABSORBER, Drive Line Vib.  It was included on 86+ cars and located on the front right hand side (pass.) of the cradle.  I had heard some time ago that this part is/was discontinued.  It is actually a very small shock absorber.  I think it is even fluid filled, but I don't know that for a fact.  These guys are apparently talking about the STRUT, Engine Mounting, or in laymens terms the dogbone.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000014||Monkeyman||12-17-1999||11:37 PM||||I've got to get a 22P, but I understand what you're saying.  Thanx for the clarification.||||reg||1
Z||000015||theogre||12-18-1999||01:43 PM||||The "dog bone" is one type of torque reaction strut. It is the only type use in 4 cyl, and 88 v6. The hydraulic unit is another type and is designed to work in a diferant range of vibration conditions. In the case of Fiero V6 it is also configured to transmit it's load to the cradle. This reduces the vibration sent directly to the body by the "bone".<P>I'm currently working on some things for the 4 cyl that will likely be transferable to the V6. ((There are some problems with the 4 cyl/auto tranny setup after 86.5. They are related to the use of only one tranny mount. The OEM bone is to soft for this setup but the polly transmits all the vibration. I'm planning to return to the dual mounts for the tranny use pre 86.5 and/or relocating the bone to load to the craddle.))<P>I haven't seen a V6 in awhile. I can't remember exactly how the hydro strut is setup or it's size. It may be entirely posible to replace that strut with a polly bushed solid bone and eliminate the upper. Many cars that were built with hydro units in an upper position latter replaced them with solids. Even the replacement you buy, from say NAPA or Autozone, for many hydro struts is solid.<P>Problem with the lowwer V6 hydro is that it may be too low or angled wrong to be effective. Has anyone examined the mounts used with the V6 in say a cavalier Z24? Those may give some insight I don't presently have available.<P>As for removing the upper strut from anny Fiero, even leaving the lower hydro on a V6, DON'T. You will break things. Not just motor mounts. I said this before, but I've seen the kind of damage it can do. It can literally totaly your car. The hydro unit in the V6 is designed to dampen small vibration. It doesn't hold the motor in place. The hydro unit will allow up to several inches of travel in the upper area of the motor.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-18-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Monkeyman||12-18-1999||06:16 PM||||ogre--I followed everything in your 1st sentence.  Please explain everything else to me as if I were a 6th grader.||||reg||1
Z||000017||batboy||12-18-1999||10:40 PM||||He says don't remove the dog bone. Bad things happen if you do.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 12-18-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Monkeyman||12-19-1999||01:12 AM||||Thanx.  That pretty much explains it. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000019||DJRice||12-20-1999||10:52 AM||||Before I discovered this forum, I went hunting a new dog bone, and it took the parts guy about 20 minutes and several trips to his supply shelves to decide that I didnt want the "upper motor mount" as I was asking for.  I wanted a "Torque reaction strut."  Of course it was $50 and I told him not to bother ordering it for me. <P>Actually, I need to put a new bushing in that "lower torque reaction strut shock absorber thingy" in my 85 v6.  <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  ||||reg||1
